 player  shadows and being able to see a player  shadow is key to tracking opponents  indoors and anticipating enemy movement  it can make the difference between death  and survival also note that high and  very high will tax your system heavily  foliage changes the detail of brush and  grass but it does not affect the amount  that shows up in game it also doesn't  affect the distance at which grass pops  up so you won't really gain an advantage  by turning it down  the  this should be maxed out to make sure  you can see as much as possible off in  the distance it's key if you're using  high-powered scopes or trying to watch  activity from a high vantage point  post-processing encompasses effects like  ambient occlusion and dynamic lighting  low will give you ambient occlusion  which is a notable visual improvement I  will add a sort of depth of field and  very high will add dynamic lighting  effects like bloom and godrays effects  determines the number of particle used  when rendering visual effects like  gunfire and explosions the more the  better but your system will take a  performance hit when fire and explosions  fill up the screen texture improves the  detail of surfaces simple enough but  we'd suggest that least using medium  since low is a very noticeable downgrade  screen scale is super sampling if it's  set above 100 the game will render a  higher res image and downscale to fit  your display while it makes the game  look better I'd avoid using it in this  case motion blur is up to your  preference and you'll want to turn vsync  off now with our modest system and  preferred settings you're probably  wondering what kind of frame rates we  got through an entire match the frame  rate stayed between the high 50s to the  high 70s it seems that with our system  and settings we hit a sweet spot between  graphical quality and performance during  an airstrike barrage our frame rate  dipped to the low 40s with effects set  to high bringing it down to medium  helped 